{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T21:11:19.182Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mod-ecosystem-research","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"task","args":{"description":"Research mods and libraries","items":["Botania — https://github.com/VazkiiMods/Botania (mod). A large, long-lived magic/tech mod by Vazkii, famous for data-driven content and clean architecture.","CoFH Thermal series — https://github.com/CoFH (this is a GitHub ORG, not a single repo; core repos include ThermalExpansion, ThermalFoundation, ThermalDynamics, CoFHCore). Cover the org-level structure and how the core + expansions mod split works.","BiggerReactors — https://github.com/BiggerSeries/BiggerReactors (mod). Multiblock reactors, originally a Big Reactors revival.","EnderIO — https://github.com/Team-EnderIO (this is a GitHub ORG; the core repo is EnderIO, with related repos like EnderIO-Monorepo or addon repos — verify the current structure). Conduits and machines.","HarvestCraft — https://github.com/MatrexsVigil/harvestcraft (mod). IMPORTANT: this upstream is stale Forge-era (1.12.2). Research the historical repo, AND identify the current maintained successor (e.g., Pam's HarvestCraft 2 on CurseForge or any newer GitHub repo) — report both, clearly separated.","Minecraft Comes Alive (MCA) — https://github.com/WildBamaBoy/minecraft-comes-alive (mod). IMPORTANT: this upstream is stale (1.12-era). Research the historical repo, AND find the maintained continuation (MCA Reborn — locate its actual GitHub repo and current Minecraft/loader versions) — report both, clearly separated.","GeckoLib — https://github.com/bernie-g/geckolib and its wiki at https://github.com/bernie-g/geckolib/wiki (library). The de-facto animation/entity-model library for modded Minecraft.","Bookshelf — https://github.com/Darkhax-Minecraft/Bookshelf (library). PRIORITY TARGET — Darkhax's widely-used utility library; cover its helper APIs (registries, data, networking, etc.) at the level of 'what problems it solves'.","Cloth Config — docs at https://shedaniel.gitbook.io/cloth-config and repo https://github.com/shedaniel/cloth-config (library). The de-facto standard for in-game config screens."],"prompt_template":"You are researching one open-source Minecraft mod/library to build a durable reference page for the `siasworkshop` project — a new NeoForge mod for Minecraft 1.21.1 (NeoForge 21.1.256, Java 21, ModDevGradle 2.x, Parchment mappings). The mod will live in the same genre space as classic tech/magic/farming/villager mods, and the team wants a curated map of where to look for examples and orientation. Your target:\n\n{{item}}\n\n## Your task\n\nResearch the target using the web (GitHub repo pages, READMEs, build files, source-tree layout, branches/releases/gradle.properties, official docs/wikis, CurseForge/Modrinth pages where relevant). Do NOT deep-dive individual source files — a structural/architectural survey is what we need. Verify version and loader claims from repo artifacts (branches, tags, gradle.properties, build.gradle) rather than trusting memory. If a fact cannot be verified, mark it `(unverified)`.\n\n## Output format\n\nReturn ONLY a markdown body (no frontmatter, no preamble, no trailing notes), at most ~450 words, terse bullets, no code dumps. Use exactly these section headings (for a MOD):\n\n## What it is\n## Status on MC 1.21.1 / NeoForge\n## License\n## Architecture and repo layout\n## Practices worth borrowing\n## Watch-outs\n## Key links\n\nFor a LIBRARY, use exactly these headings instead:\n\n## What it is\n## NeoForge 1.21.1 support\n## License\n## Core API in a nutshell\n## Adding it as a dependency\n## Who uses it / practices worth borrowing\n## Watch-outs\n## Key links\n\nContent guidance:\n- \"Status\" sections: which MC versions and mod loaders the current release/branch actually supports; whether a 1.21.1 NeoForge build exists, is in progress, or is absent. Be precise — this decides whether siasworkshop can borrow code directly or only take inspiration.\n- \"License\": name the exact license from the repo's LICENSE file (e.g., MIT, GPL-3.0, custom). If custom, summarize its redistribution/code-borrowing conditions in one line.\n- \"Architecture and repo layout\": package/module organization, how registration is done (DeferredRegister vs other), build system, notable patterns (data-driven JSON, capabilities, custom rendering, networking).\n- \"Practices worth borrowing\": 3–6 concrete, named patterns an agent could go study (point at specific packages/classes/files with repo-relative paths).\n- \"Watch-outs\": license gotchas, legacy-version traps, heavy abstractions, anything that bites.\n- \"Key links\": 3–6 URLs (repo, docs/wiki, maven/curseforge/modrinth as appropriate).\n- For stale-upstream mods asked to also find the maintained successor: give the successor its own clearly-labeled subsection under \"Status\" with its repo URL and current supported versions.","subagent_type":"explore"}}}
